# Daily product-in-use Pinterest pins

The user approved the All-Clad brownie example and requested one product-in-use Pinterest pin every day, in addition to the existing recipe-pinning workflow.

- Read the product-to-recipe mappings from `src/lib/amazon-affiliates.ts`; use published full recipes only.
- Use the exact product's existing photograph as the image reference. Show it recognizably in use for the paired recipe.
- Create a dedicated 9:16 image, 1080x1920 JPG. Include recipe title, product name, GrandmaRecipe.com and a readable AI-generated illustration label.
- The title and description must connect the actual product to the recipe. Do not imply hands-on testing or brand endorsement.
- Start the description with #ad, include an affiliate disclosure, and end with `Get the full recipe on Grandma Recipe.`
- Link to the recipe page, where the matching Amazon affiliate link appears. No prices, ratings, pork or alcohol.
- Keep the existing publish-on-release and old-recipe catch-up schedules intact.
- Product pins have their own `pinterest-product-pins/` packs, public mirrors, queue and posted log. Limit to one product pin per Casablanca calendar day.
- The user clarified on November 28 that previously pinned articles SHOULD get these new Amazon product-in-use pins too. Ordinary recipe pin history does not exclude a recipe from the product campaign.
- Exclude recipe/product pairs already recorded in `pinterest-product-pins/.posted.json`, including renamed/redirected versions. Keep the one-product-pin-per-day limit. `account-history.json` is an audit of ordinary recipe pins, not a product-campaign exclusion list.
- Prepare new images in batches of 30. The user authorized adding all 30 existing product drafts to the four queued pins, for 34 upcoming product pins.
- Prepare images here with the built-in image generator, using exact product reference photos. The scheduled GitHub workflow posts saved images only, as the old recipe backlog did. Do not add a paid image API, image-generation dependency or API secret.
- Follow `pinterest-product-pins/settings.json`. Keep a clear count of ready images and remaining recipes; never describe pending-image entries as ready-to-post pins.
- The brownie example was posted on 2026-11-28: https://www.pinterest.com/pin/1137229343447595240/.
